---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ChangeImageDpi from '@/components/tools/image-edit/ChangeImageDpi.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="change-image-dpi">
  <ChangeImageDpi client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What DPI actually is</h2>
    <p>
      DPI (dots per inch, sometimes PPI) is a label, not a property of the image data. It tells layout
      software how big one pixel should be on paper. A 600 × 600 pixel image tagged 300 DPI prints at
      2 × 2 inches; the same file tagged 72 DPI prints at 8.3 inches and looks blurry, with no change to
      any pixel. A change of the tag does not change the sharpness of the file, only the intended
      physical size.
    </p>
    <p>
      For this reason, setting DPI here is instant and lossless: the tool changes the density field in
      the file header and does not touch the compressed image data.
    </p>

    <h2>So why do forms demand 300 DPI?</h2>
    <p>
      There are two reasons. First, layout software reads the tag to place the image, and a file
      without a tag defaults to 72 DPI. The image is then placed more than four times too large, and
      the reviewer sees a soft, oversized picture. Second, submission guidelines use “300 DPI” to mean
      “enough pixels to print well”, but the tag alone cannot give that. Set the tag for the checker,
      and check the pixel count for the printer.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the number is stored</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Format</th><th>Field</th><th>Units</th><th>Can the tool write it?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>JPEG</td><td>JFIF APP0 segment: units, Xdensity, Ydensity</td><td>Per inch or per cm</td><td>Yes: changed in place, or inserted if absent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>JPEG with EXIF</td><td>IFD0 tags XResolution (0x011A), YResolution (0x011B), ResolutionUnit (0x0128)</td><td>Rational, inch or cm</td><td>Yes: updated so it agrees with JFIF</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PNG</td><td><code>pHYs</code> chunk</td><td>Pixels per metre</td><td>Yes: rewritten or inserted after IHDR, with a new CRC32</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WebP, GIF, BMP</td><td>No standard density field</td><td>n/a</td><td>No: convert to JPG or PNG first</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The tool writes both JPEG fields because some applications read JFIF and others prefer EXIF. A
      file where the two disagree lays out differently in different applications.
    </p>

    <h2>Set DPI only, or resample?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Set DPI only: the tool rewrites the header and nothing else. Use this when the pixel count is already correct and a form or a designer needs the tag to say 300. It is instant and cannot degrade the image, because the image data is not touched.</li>
      <li>Resample: you give the physical size and the DPI. The tool calculates the pixel dimensions, resizes to them with a Lanczos3 filter, and writes the tag. Use this when the printed size must be exact. It re-encodes, so it is not lossless, and it warns you when the target needs upscaling.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The arithmetic, both ways</h2>
    <p>
      Pixels = inches × DPI, and inches = pixels ÷ DPI. A 4 × 6 inch print at 300 DPI needs
      1200 × 1800 pixels, and a 3000 × 2000 pixel photo tagged 300 DPI prints at 10 × 6.67 inches. The
      table on the page does this for your image at each common resolution, in inches and centimetres,
      so you can see if your file is big enough for your print.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a resolution</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>DPI</th><th>Use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>72 / 96</td><td>Old screen defaults. Still the fallback when a file has no tag.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>150</td><td>Draft printing, newspapers, large posters seen from a distance.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>200</td><td>Everyday office laser output; often the practical limit of the paper.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>300</td><td>The standard for photo prints, passport photos, magazines and most submission guidelines.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>600</td><td>Line art, engravings, fine text, high-quality scans.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1200</td><td>Archival scanning and reprographics. Very large files; seldom necessary for photographs.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Common misconceptions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>“Changing DPI to 300 will make my photo higher quality.” No. It makes the printed version smaller, which can look sharper on paper, but adds no detail.</li>
      <li>“My 72 DPI image is low resolution.” The tag says nothing about resolution. A 6000 × 4000 photo tagged 72 DPI has exactly the same detail as the same file tagged 300.</li>
      <li>“DPI affects how it looks on screen.” Browsers and most viewers ignore it and lay images out in pixels. It matters for print and in page-layout software.</li>
      <li>“Resampling up to 300 DPI fixes a small image.” It only invents pixels by interpolation. The tool tells you when your settings do this.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/passport-photo-maker">Passport Photo Maker</a> writes 300 DPI automatically for
      document photos, <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> changes pixel dimensions directly,
      and <a href="/image-metadata-viewer">Image Metadata Viewer</a> shows the full EXIF block,
      including the resolution tags this tool writes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
